<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roger I of Sicily</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1031–1101</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Roger de Hauteville, Great Count Roger</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roger I was a Norman adventurer of the Hauteville family, youngest brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a>, who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from its Muslim rulers. He crossed the Strait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> in 1061, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> with Robert in 1072 and completed the conquest in 1091, ruling as Count of Sicily. He governed a mixed Greek, Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> population with tolerance, and his son Roger II became the first King of Sicily.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roger-i-of-sicily.jpg" alt="Roger I of Sicily" width="320" height="331" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: sailko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calabria,_trifollaro_di_ruggieri_I_d%27altavilla,_1072-1101.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Roger was born in Normandy, probably in Hauteville-la-Guichard, the youngest son of Tancred de Hauteville and his second wife Fressenda. Little is known of his life before the expeditions to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where he arrived as a young man in the summer of 1057. His brother Robert inherited the main title, and Roger became his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> as holder of the newly conquered County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>. The monk Goffredo Malaterra described him as a youth of great beauty, eloquent in speech and cool in counsel. He established his court at Mileto.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In May 1061 the brothers crossed from Reggio and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a>, invited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-thumna">Ibn al-Thumna</a>, emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catania</a>, who was at war with his brother-in-law, the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agrigento">Agrigento</a>. In June 1063 Roger defeated a Muslim army at the Battle of Cerami, and he won another victory at Misilmeri in 1068. In 1071 he was made Grand Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and on 10 January 1072 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> was taken after a siege of five months.</p><p>In 1086 he attacked Syracuse, where the emir Benavert drowned in the night attack on his fleet, and the city surrendered in October. Agrigento fell in 1087, Butera in 1090, and by 1091 Roger controlled the whole island. That year he also took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, whose qadi agreed to continue administering the islands as a tributary of the count.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Roger made himself master of the island's church. In 1098 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> granted him and his heirs the apostolic legateship, and he created new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> bishoprics at Syracuse, Girgenti and elsewhere. He practised general toleration towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and sponsored over twelve Greek monasteries. In the cities Muslims who had secured such terms kept their mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a> and freedom of trade, but in the countryside they became serfs, and Roger drew most of his infantry from them. No feudal revolt of importance arose against him.</p><p>Roger died on 22 June 1101 at Mileto and was buried at the Benedictine Abbey of the Holy Trinity. His son Simon became count with his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-del-vasto">Adelaide del Vasto</a> as regent. After Simon died in 1105, aged 12, the title passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roger I of Sicily” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Normans under <b>Roger de Hauteville</b> then invaded.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the eleventh century Norman adventurers led by the Hauteville family took Apulia and Calabria from the Byzantines and Lombard princes, and between 1061 and 1091 Count <b>Roger I</b> conquered Sicily from its Muslim emirs, taking Palermo in 1072 together with his brother Robert Guiscard.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Roger de Hauteville</b>, Robert Guiscard's youngest brother, crossed to Sicily in 1061 and took Messina.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-sicily">County of Sicily</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hauteville-la-Guichard, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Mileto, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.13,-1.3&amp;year=1076" aria-label="Hauteville-la-Guichard on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4520 450 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Roger I of Sicily"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4970 720h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4970 720h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.13,-1.3&amp;year=1076">What was here in 1076? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
